WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:02.040
If you haven't noticed lately,

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.773
we're living in the future.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.540
Technology that was only imaginable

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.880
in science fiction is now so mundane

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.880
that we practically take it for granted.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:12.970
But there's still one thing

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:16.580
that's conspicuously absent
from our futuristic present.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.300
Where are the flying cars?

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.110
I was promised flying cars.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.000
I don't see any flying cars.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.360
Why, why, why?

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.210
The one thing that both utopian

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.200
and dystopian visions of our future

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.340
have seemed to agree on is that by now

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.350
we'd be flying around our cities.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:33.740
But for the vast majority of us

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.040
who don't happen to own
private helicopters,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.880
we still mostly move around
at or below ground level,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.433
often stuck in congestion.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:43.920
But over the last decade,

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.560
a growing cadre of bootstrapping startups

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.210
and industry stalwarts

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.080
have been raising and
spending oodles of money

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.260
on a new mode of transportation
known as urban air mobility.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.410
Could these autonomous,
human-ferrying super drones

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.193
finally be the flying cars
that we've been waiting for?

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.640
I think there are two things

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.070
that are driving the investment

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.520
and the attention to the sector.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.020
The fact that people are moving to cities,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.830
and cities are getting more congested,

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:20.970
and urban dwellers do
not want to own cars.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.800
So anything that can move an urban dweller

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.270
from A to B is interesting to investors.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.590
We saw it with micro mobility solutions.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.920
We saw it with shared mobility solutions.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.603
The next natural step is
shared aviation solutions.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.870
The concept of urban air mobility

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.180
isn't totally new.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.970
For decades, helicopters have been a means

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.220
for those with means to
hover and glide over traffic

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.330
in major urban centers like
New York, San Francisco,

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:47.173
and Sao Paolo.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.920
Past attempts at turning helicopters

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.250
into a form of mass transit, however,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.980
have been grounded, mostly
by the inevitable problems

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.800
of helicopter technology,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.343
noise, inefficiency, and danger.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.010
Thanks to these issues,

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.530
many cities have either limited

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.250
or banned helicopter transit outright,

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.903
except for limited use,
such as in emergencies.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.150
But imagine a future which
instead of driving your own car

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.100
or hopping on a city
bus or subway or taxi,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.220
you could cut your
transit time significantly

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.660
by booking a flight on a sleek, safe,

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.140
and relatively quiet electric
powered drone, or EVTOL,

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.040
short for Electric Vertical
Takeoff and Landing.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:33.040
If we look at just 0.1% of the Americans

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.600
that are driving in their
cars from work to home,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.740
and we assume that's going
to be our addressable market,

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.520
we're talking about a
100,000 passengers a year,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.460
more or less, that need to be moved.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.190
If we assume that these aircraft can carry

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.210
about four to five people,

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.370
and they're doing four
to five trips a day,

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:55.500
we would need roughly
5,000 of these aircraft.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:56.560
So that gives you a sense

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.180
of the massive opportunity we have here.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.740
Given this potential market,

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:04.130
the field is unsurprisingly very crowded.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:05.850
There are over 180 companies

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.330
developing some type of EVTOL.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.660
A lot of them are drone developers.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:11.820
So it's all about scale.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.220
If I can build a small version,

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.670
I should be able to scale
it to a larger version.

00:03:20.377 --> 00:03:22.326
One of these drone turned megacompanies

00:03:22.326 --> 00:03:25.060
is EHang, a Chinese firm founded in 2014

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.490
that has already earned a spot on NASDAQ.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.380
From the very beginning,

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:33.020
we designed big drones to
carry passengers, right?

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:36.490
And second, you can see
that for normal drones,

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.600
the technology is one-on-one.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:43.240
That means we need one
person to control each drone,

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.950
but for us, we designed the system

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:50.950
to control many, multiple
AAVs simultaneously

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.963
by using one single platform.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.460
EHang recently secured approval

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.890
from the Korean government
to perform test flights

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.210
over Seoul, showing off
the company's prototype

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.280
during a recent three-hour tour.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.220
Although EHang is designing
its remote controlled prototype

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.300
to fit applications ranging
from medical evacuations

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:14.290
to sightseeing, it's the
so-called last 10 mile problem

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:16.500
that seems to be the
most ripe for disruption.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.973
For example, if you take a
train to arrive in Beijing,

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:25.980
the train takes about
one or two hours, right?

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.460
And after the train, if you take a taxi,

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.530
it takes another one
hour or even two hours

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:36.410
to go to the destination,
which doesn't make any sense.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:40.350
But if you can transfer our AAV,

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:45.350
it takes maybe 15 minutes to
arrive at at your destination.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:46.863
That's very cool.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.250
Meanwhile, on the other side of the globe,

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.940
a German company called Volocopter

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.490
has spent the last decade
or so designing a fleet

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.530
of prototypes that have
recently been flying

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:01.180
in demonstrations over cities
both at home and abroad.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:02.740
We are extremely pragmatic.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:05.710
We set out to build a
vehicle that can meet

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:07.310
the high safety standards,

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.020
that can be certified,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.820
based on existing technologies
that are available today.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.490
What came out is the Volocopter.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:20.300
We explicitly designed it
as a multicopter concept,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.050
so we have 18 independent rotors

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:27.260
that give us a very high
degree of redundancy.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.540
So any two, any three rotors can fail

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.680
and we can still safely
compete our mission,

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.620
which gives us huge safety
advantage over any other concept.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.240
And particularly on the urban mission,

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:40.070
where it's about safely
transporting people

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:44.080
from A to B in an urban
context, as quiet as possible,

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.900
we believe the multicopter
concept is the winning design

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.350
for that particular mission.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:49.780
There may be other missions that require

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.460
different design concepts.

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.210
Volocopter's ambitious plans

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.730
have attracted interest from
a number of large companies

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.800
hailing from other sectors
of the transportation world,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.550
such as Chinese auto giant
Geely, Germany's Daimler,

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.870
and most recently Japan Airlines,

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:07.070
all of whom have participated
in funding rounds

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:08.780
for Volocopter that have raised

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.263
well over a hundred million euros so far.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.683
Of all the companies
currently working on EVTOLs,

00:06:14.683 --> 00:06:16.820
Volocopter seems to be the furthest along

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:18.270
in committing to a launch date,

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.070
already taking reservations for flights

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.170
beginning as soon as 2023

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:25.103
at an introductory price
of around 300 euros.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:28.360
But the main hurdle that
could disrupt that timeline

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.340
isn't merely a technical one.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:33.680
This is a very safety
critical transportation mode,

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:34.810
and you need to make sure

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:36.930
that you have all relevant
stakeholders involved.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.100
So we are really relying on the buy-in

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.430
of the regulators and gaining their trust.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.830
So we always talk to them and be like,

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.780
okay, do we have an alignment of vision?

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.030
Do we have the civil
aviation authority on board?

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:49.300
Do we have the airspace manager on board?

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:50.680
You need to make sure
that you have alignment,

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.970
because any one of those
stakeholders can block you

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:55.033
from ultimately achieving your goal.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.080
Part of the added challenge

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.550
in gaining regulatory approval

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:02.180
for civilian carrying EVTOLs
is that the standards issued

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.330
by regulatory agencies are evolving

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:06.563
in tandem with the technology itself.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:10.920
They have to be airworthy, these aircraft.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.230
They're gonna be running on batteries,

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:16.300
and batteries need to be also approved

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.250
as safe enough to be in an aircraft

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:19.850
for this particular application.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:24.380
Volocopter, EHang,

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.380
and other well-heeled EVTOLers,

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:28.230
such as Lilium and Joby,

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:30.030
are each taking a slightly
different approach

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:33.430
to engineering an
all-electric passenger drone.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:35.210
Some of the designs are more of a hybrid

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.680
between an EVTOL and a small plane,

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.710
capable of both navigating
a dense urban environment

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:42.080
and flying long distances

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.033
at up to almost 200 miles per hour.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:46.740
And while some of the
engineering solutions

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.190
for challenges like battery
capacity are pretty innovative,

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:53.660
such as Volocopter's hot
swapping charging stations,

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:55.710
any EVTOL's viability

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.670
ultimately comes down
to proving its safety

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:59.933
in a variety of conditions and scenarios.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:03.320
So assuming that EVTOL technology

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:06.170
eventually passes those
regulatory hurdles,

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:07.920
that still leaves a
very important question

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.930
about this untested market.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.323
Who exactly are these for?

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:16.670
If we look at Elon Musk's master plan,

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:20.180
the first iteration in
2006, he was very clear.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:22.690
He said he was going to
build an expensive car

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:25.520
to help pay for a less expensive car

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.780
to help pay for a mass market car.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:30.630
So I think we're gonna see
something quite similar

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.630
in this specific segment,

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:35.290
where companies will
be offering the service

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:38.750
to high-end consumers, but in the end

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:40.590
that will subsidize the technology

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.040
so that everybody can
access it in the long term.

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:46.680
Mass adoption of urban
air mobility, though,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.660
might pose its own potential issues.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.750
Once the product trickles down

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.170
and more people start using it,

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:55.310
we fear it might actually
cannibalize users

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:57.140
from public transit in cities,

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:00.870
which will have negative
consequences in our view,

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.610
mainly because it will
just shift congestion

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.313
from the road up into the sky.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:07.410
Before we get to that point, though,

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.940
these companies face yet
another technical challenge,

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.670
integrating urban air mobility

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.270
into the existing
transportation landscape.

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.340
On this front, Uber is taking advantage

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:17.750
of their own learned experience

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.510
building a revolutionary
ride sharing platform

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:23.340
to bring their technology
into a third dimension,

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:26.650
and they're not even waiting
for EVTOLs to get started.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:30.440
Back in the quaint
pre-pandemic days of late 2019,

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:32.490
I gave their Uber Copter service a whirl.

00:09:33.576 --> 00:09:34.470
There is no smoking allowed.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.676
All right, we're en route to JFK.

00:09:36.676 --> 00:09:38.920
I can take this to work every day.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:42.230
We're really thinking of
this as kind of a version 0.1

00:09:43.123 --> 00:09:45.260
of our vision of this
future Uber air product.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:46.220
The key thing what we think

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:51.060
is the important learning that
we are developing right now

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.040
is this foundational feature,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.230
kind of this core
experience of multimodality.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:57.600
So true multimodality.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.430
So with a single button
press for the product,

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:03.400
we dispatch the first
mile car, the helicopter,

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:05.290
and the last mile car bi-directionally

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:09.980
from downtown to to JFK, and
we're using our technology

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.630
to do that all behind the scenes.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.780
Admittedly, it's extremely satisfying

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.400
to fly above New York City's
snarling rush hour traffic

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.280
and plop down on the
landing pad at JFK Airport

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.330
within 10 minutes,

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:23.980
where a car is waiting to shuttle you

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:25.333
directly to your terminal.

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:29.410
But even Uber understands that
once the novelty wears off,

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:31.100
it needs to make economic sense

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.340
in order for customers to adopt it.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:35.080
The economics of these
vehicles will allow us

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.470
to push and bring it to be a
much more accessible product

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:42.170
that a broad base of users
can use on a daily basis.

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:43.890
We actually think the long-term vision,

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:46.430
we can actually get to
the point where flying

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:49.440
in these types of aerial
networks can be competitive

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:51.700
with driving your car, with owning a car,

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:53.630
in terms of like the
marginal cost of ownership.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:54.910
It's not gonna happen right away.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:56.350
It's not gonna happen in the medium-term,

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:57.630
but we think in the long run

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:01.700
that we can actually make
flying in this type of a fashion

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:03.710
kind of a economically rational thing

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:05.260
for most people to do everyday.

00:11:06.821 --> 00:11:08.040
Despite the sleek renderings

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:11.060
of swanky rooftop
heliports and city skylines

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:13.910
buzzing with EVTOLs, the commercial case

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:17.260
for urban air mobility
is still up in the air,

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.960
putting pressure on the
companies vying for viability.

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.190
Given the complexities of the solution,

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:25.640
the complexities of the regulation,

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:29.840
we do not expect to see
a very diverse ecosystem

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:31.000
in the long term.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.050
We rather expect to see a
few, a handful of developers,

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:37.540
of manufacturers, working with a handful

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:39.823
of service providers.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:42.370
And that consolidation

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:44.550
seems to already be underway.

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:48.120
In late 2020, Uber handed off
its ambitious Elevate project

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.620
to Joby, the current EVTOL valuation king,

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:54.473
with nearly $1 billion to its name so far.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:57.410
Boeing and Airbus seem to be stepping back

00:11:57.410 --> 00:11:59.720
from their experiments as well.

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:02.340
Some of the smaller startups
have vanished entirely,

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:04.330
leaving behind expired domain names

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.260
and silent Crunchbase profiles.

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:08.840
So as the field narrows,

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:10.600
the technology matures,

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.000
and the regulations are ironed out,

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:16.720
you may someday find that
this perennial sci-fi fantasy

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:19.470
could become another mundane
option for getting around.

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:23.060
The way that we envision this to play out

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:25.520
is ultimately the customer,
whether it's a citizen

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:27.680
in the city or a visitor to the city,

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:30.310
has their mobile phone,
has a mobility need,

